## What I changed

The `membershipNumber` is derived from the owner's own fields (`customerCode` and `registrationDate`), so I exposed it as a read-only response field computed at mapping time — no changes to the entity, service, or persistence layer.

**Two files touched:**

1. **`openapi.yml`** — added a read-only `membershipNumber` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.

2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** — added one `@Mapping` annotation to `toOwnerDto` plus a small, self-contained `membershipNumber(Owner)` default method:
   ```java
   default String membershipNumber(Owner owner) {
       if (owner.getCustomerCode() == null || owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {
           return null;
       }
       return String.format("%s-M%02d", owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100);
   }
   ```

## Why this keeps structural impact low

- I put the logic in `OwnerMapper` (a small interface, low `WMC_other`) rather than in `Owner` (a large class whose ~35 combined CC would multiply every edit).
- The behaviour is a new, cohesive single-CC-branch helper; the only existing method changed is `toOwnerDto`, which gets one annotation line.
- The null guard makes it correct even for seed owners (whose `customer_code`/`registration_date` are `NULL`), avoiding NPEs while producing `SMI-0007-M26`-style values for created owners (`year % 100`, zero-padded to two digits).

